1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:02,880
I want to set aside a Bitcoin prize

2
00:00:02,880 --> 00:00:05,120
before I know the winner’s Bitcoin address.

3
00:00:05,440 --> 00:00:07,200
That’s why I built PrizeKey.

4
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:09,300
Here is a funded gift.

5
00:00:09,560 --> 00:00:11,660
I check its balance and assigned account,

6
00:00:11,780 --> 00:00:13,700
then open the recipient page.

7
00:00:21,800 --> 00:00:23,740
Using the existing assigned account,

8
00:00:23,940 --> 00:00:25,940
I paste a Bitcoin test address

9
00:00:25,940 --> 00:00:27,450
and review the payout.

10
00:00:27,640 --> 00:00:30,900
That’s 99,750 test sats,

11
00:00:30,900 --> 00:00:33,200
with a 250-sat fee.

12
00:00:33,480 --> 00:00:34,800
I stop before approval.

13
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:40,780
To show a completed handoff,

14
00:00:40,960 --> 00:00:43,500
this is the earlier 20,000-sat gift.

15
00:00:43,800 --> 00:00:45,740
I enter the winner’s NEAR account

16
00:00:45,740 --> 00:00:47,300
and transfer the claim.

17
00:00:50,900 --> 00:00:53,560
The new holder chooses a Bitcoin destination

18
00:00:53,560 --> 00:00:55,240
and authorizes through

19
00:00:55,240 --> 00:00:56,600
NEAR Chain Signatures.

20
00:00:57,000 --> 00:00:58,850
The browser broadcasts Bitcoin.

21
00:00:59,320 --> 00:01:00,640
This recorded run used

22
00:01:00,640 --> 00:01:02,200
terminal wallet approval.

23
00:01:03,300 --> 00:01:04,900
Back in the current phone app,

24
00:01:05,140 --> 00:01:09,180
that gift shows 19,750 test sats delivered,

25
00:01:09,180 --> 00:01:10,900
and the assigned recipient.

26
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:15,120
I open its public record to check the payout.

27
00:01:15,240 --> 00:01:16,600
Fund Bitcoin first.

28
00:01:16,880 --> 00:01:18,280
Choose the recipient later.

29
00:01:18,580 --> 00:01:20,400
This is still a testnet prototype.
